      "id": 1,
      "text": "Pryorsburg takes its name from the Pryor family, early landowners who helped shape the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/pryorsburg_kentucky",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The New Orleans and Ohio Railroad arrived in 1854, sparking rapid growth along Obion Creek and turning Pryorsburg into a bustling depot.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/pryorsburg_kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "An accidental discovery of soft clay near Pryorsburg in 1891 ignited the region’s clay and ceramic industry throughout the 1890s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/pryorsburg_kentucky",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Pryorsburg lies on flat to gently rolling terrain typical of the Jackson Purchase region in western Kentucky, within the Mississippi Embayment.",
